// `concat` is one name over three forms, chosen by the first argument: pieces into a string, parts
// into a new array, parts appended to a MutableArray in place. It was three names -- `concat`,
// `concat_arrays`, `mutable_array_push_all` -- until 2026-10-18.
//
// **One argument that is an array spreads.** That rule is what keeps `concat(parts)` answering the
// string "xyz" for a `[string]`, which it did when the string form was a native with a variadic
// tail, and it is the reason concatenating a single array *as one part* cannot be said: there is no
// way to ask for a copy of a `[string]` through `concat`, and `slice(a, 0, len(a))` is that anyway.
// A lone array whose elements cannot be pieces or parts -- a `[int]` -- is the one part, so
// `concat([1, 2])` is a copy. Two or more arguments are never a spread.
fn main() {
    // pieces into a string
    print(concat("a", "b", "c"))
    print(concat("solo"))
    print(concat())
    val words: [string] = ["x", "y", "z"]
    print(concat(words))

    // parts into an array
    print(concat([1, 2], [3], [4, 5]))
    val nested: [[int]] = [[1, 2], [3]]
    print(concat(nested))
    print(concat([1, 2]))
    print(concat(["a"], ["b"]))

    // appended to a builder, either world, mixed, any number
    val b = mutable([1])
    concat(b, [2, 3])
    concat(b, mutable([4]))
    concat(b, [5], mutable([6]), [7])
    print(snapshot(b))

    // self-aliasing: doubles, and several aliasing parts append sequentially
    val c = mutable([1, 2])
    concat(c, c)
    print(snapshot(c))
    val d = mutable([1])
    concat(d, d, d)
    print(snapshot(d))
    print(concat([1, 2], [1, 2]))
}
